  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1032 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows another shortwave trough lifting\nnorthwards from northern Chihuahua. Radar shows an expansive area\nof light to moderate rain, with embedded heavier thunderstorms\nsouth of the border, but IR imagery shows cloud tops are warming.\nCAMS have all been having a rough time all day keeping up with\nwhat is actually going on. The 00Z RRFS weakens most of the precip\nas it lifts north, except around the El Paso area after 06Z. The\n03Z HRRR seems to have the best handle on present reality, and\nconcurs with weakening most of the precip band except for near an\nMCV that lifts up over El Paso and into the Sacramento Mountains\nbetween Midnight and 5 AM. We've also been seeing some scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms breaking out over the El Paso area in\nthe past 45 minutes or so, perhaps associated with a strengthening\nsubtropical jet streak that should be nosing into the area about\nnow, per the 00Z GFS. Another focus for showers and thunderstorms\nshould be near the Hudspeth/Culberson county line, but so far this\nband has been fairly weak. \n\nHeading into tomorrow (Thursday), we'll be on the backside of the\nshortwave for most of the day, then the next wave arrives with \nincreasing thunderstorm coverage from the southwest in the evening\nand overnight hours. Areas east of the Continental Divide appear \nmost favored, and near/east of the Rio Grande. We'll need to \nclosely watch the westward edge, as there's a chance heavy \nrainfall may strafe Luna County and up towards the Black Range, \nwest of the continuing portion of the Flood Watch. The already- \nsaturated ground around El Paso and south-central NM, as well as \nthe Sacramento Mountains, are obviously a concern.\n\nDrier air will filter in across southwestern New Mexico on Friday,\nwith lingering shower/thunderstorm chances further east. PWAT\nvalues will be on the downtrend, and a more west to east storm\nmotion will be less favorable for training.\n\nQuieter for the weekend, with 30s dewpoints seeping into SW New\nMexico. Subtropical moisture returns for Monday into Tuesday.\nRemnants of Polo may come into play on Tuesday. Most track\nguidance swings Polo out west of the Baja Peninsula, when up\nacross Sonora towards New Mexico. This is usually a less favorable\nsetup for realizing the heaviest precip potential with a tropical\nsystem, with flow more westerly ahead of the storm, and more\nmountains and slightly cooler waters to cross versus coming up\nfrom the tip of Baja. Also, the jury is still out on the track,\nregardless of track guidance consensus. The trough that attempts \nto lift the tropical cyclone poleward could very well miss, \nbringing the center on more of a west-east track, or leaving it to\nwander offshore. While we really don't need the moisture, there's\nno need to hype the potential yet either.",
